973,gemma3:12b,0.0,CWE-022,True,CWE-94,"The `putObject` method is vulnerable to a path traversal vulnerability. While there's an attempt to prevent directory traversal using `path.toFile().getCanonicalPath().startsWith(root.toFile().getCanonicalPath())`, this check is insufficient because it relies on canonical paths, which can be manipulated by symbolic links. An attacker could create a symbolic link outside the intended root directory and then use that link in the `objectName` to write files outside of the bucket's designated area.",3849.96
973,gemma3:12b,0.2,CWE-022,True,CWE-94,"The `putObject` method is vulnerable to a path traversal vulnerability. While there's an attempt to prevent directory traversal using `path.toFile().getCanonicalPath().startsWith(root.toFile().getCanonicalPath())`, this check is insufficient because it relies on the canonical path, which can be manipulated by symbolic links. An attacker could create a symbolic link outside of the intended root directory and then use that link in the `objectName` to write files outside of the bucket's designated area.",2651.54
973,gemma3:12b,0.5,CWE-022,True,CWE-94,"The `putObject` method is vulnerable to path traversal. While there's an attempt to prevent directory traversal with the line `if (!path.toFile().getCanonicalPath().startsWith(root.toFile().getCanonicalPath()))`, this check relies on `getCanonicalPath()`. Canonicalization can be bypassed in certain scenarios, particularly when symbolic links are involved. An attacker could craft a malicious object name containing relative path components that, after canonicalization, still resolve to a location outside of the intended bucket root, allowing them to write files to arbitrary locations on the filesystem.",2863.14
974,gemma3:12b,0.0,CWE-022,True,CWE-94,"The `putObject` method is vulnerable to a path traversal vulnerability. While there's an attempt to prevent directory traversal using `path.toFile().getCanonicalPath().startsWith(root.toFile().getCanonicalPath())`, this check is insufficient because it relies on canonical paths, which can be manipulated by symbolic links. An attacker could create a symbolic link outside the intended root directory and then use that link in the `objectName` to write files outside of the bucket's designated area.",2606.25
974,gemma3:12b,0.2,CWE-022,True,CWE-94,"The `putObject` method is vulnerable to a path traversal vulnerability. While there's an attempt to prevent directory traversal using `path.toFile().getCanonicalPath().startsWith(root.toFile().getCanonicalPath())`, this check is insufficient because it relies on canonical paths, which can be manipulated by symbolic links. An attacker could create a symbolic link outside the intended bucket root and then specify an object name that resolves to a file outside of the bucket when combined with the symlink.",2609.17
974,gemma3:12b,0.5,CWE-022,True,CWE-94,"The `putObject` method is vulnerable to a path traversal vulnerability. While there's an attempt to prevent directory traversal with `if (!path.toFile().getCanonicalPath().startsWith(root.toFile().getCanonicalPath()))`, this check uses the `getCanonicalPath()` method on `File` objects, which can be unreliable and bypass intended restrictions in certain file systems or when symbolic links are involved.  An attacker could craft a malicious objectName that exploits these inconsistencies to write files outside of the intended bucket root directory.",2748.72
